fix(review-eval): wait for the worker pid file before forwarding a signal

The parent writes a worker's pid in the command after the one that forked it,
and Bash runs a pending trap between two commands, so a signal delivered in
that gap reached a handler that found no pid file and forwarded nothing. The
handler now waits up to a second for it — orders of magnitude more than the
gap, and still inside the parent's own grace before it KILLs the group.

The TERM test now treats a zombie as terminated. `kill -0` still succeeds on
one, and these grandchildren reparent to PID 1 when their group leader dies, so
a runner whose PID 1 reaps slowly could read a terminated process as alive. A
zombie holds nothing and spends nothing; the assertion polls for gone-or-zombie
rather than for gone.

